1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,000
And that drives you back to this mixed strategy

2
00:00:06,000 --> 00:00:07,560
and Nash equilibrium.

3
00:00:07,560 --> 00:00:10,560
A lot of people like a physical metaphor.

4
00:00:10,560 --> 00:00:14,000
So suppose that we've got a bunch of mountains here,

5
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:17,640
and we put a ball perfectly at the top.

6
00:00:17,640 --> 00:00:19,360
It's exactly balanced at the top.

7
00:00:19,360 --> 00:00:21,120
Is that an equilibrium?

8
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:21,620
Yes.

9
00:00:21,620 --> 00:00:22,120
Sure.

10
00:00:22,120 --> 00:00:23,640
Is it a stable equilibrium?

11
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:25,720
No, because if the ball goes a little to the left,

12
00:00:25,720 --> 00:00:28,040
a little bit right, you fall down to the valley.

13
00:00:28,040 --> 00:00:30,360
On the other hand, suppose we've got a ball down there.

14
00:00:30,360 --> 00:00:31,360
Is that an equilibrium?

15
00:00:31,360 --> 00:00:31,860
Yes.

16
00:00:31,860 --> 00:00:33,120
Is that a stable equilibrium?

17
00:00:33,120 --> 00:00:33,620
Yes.

18
00:00:33,620 --> 00:00:35,700
Because if you happen to get blown a little bit left

19
00:00:35,700 --> 00:00:38,680
or right, you tend to go right back to where you started.

20
00:00:38,680 --> 00:00:40,960
Of course, this doesn't mean that a really big gust

21
00:00:40,960 --> 00:00:43,360
couldn't blow you to something totally different.

22
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:45,720
But stability is about local stability.

23
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:47,440
If you just move a little bit either way,

24
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:49,920
you tend to go back or not.

25
00:00:49,920 --> 00:00:53,120
This is a physical metaphor for equilibrium,

26
00:00:53,120 --> 00:00:56,360
which is of some use.

27
00:00:56,360 --> 00:00:59,000
If you're used to thinking about physical objects,

28
00:00:59,000 --> 00:01:02,040
then that's a useful metaphor.

29
00:01:02,040 --> 00:01:04,280
On the other hand, if you just don't like doing that,

30
00:01:04,280 --> 00:01:08,360
then it's just going to confuse you more.

31
00:01:08,360 --> 00:01:10,360
Now, the intuition about this mixed strategy

32
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:12,840
and extra equilibrium is that in equilibrium,

33
00:01:12,840 --> 00:01:15,320
both strategies are equally good.

34
00:01:15,320 --> 00:01:18,320
So the hard strategy is better if you

35
00:01:18,320 --> 00:01:20,960
happen to bump into a soft bargainer,

36
00:01:20,960 --> 00:01:23,760
but it's worse if you happen to bump into a hard bargainer.

37
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:25,880
The soft bargaining strategy is better

38
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:29,520
if you happen to bump into another soft bargainer.

39
00:01:29,520 --> 00:01:32,200
You bump into a hard bargainer, then you've got a problem.

40
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:34,720
But still, it's better than bumping

41
00:01:34,720 --> 00:01:36,600
into the hard bargainer with a hard strategy,

42
00:01:36,600 --> 00:01:39,520
where you get nothing at all.

43
00:01:39,520 --> 00:01:41,440
Selenberg had a line where he says,

44
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,520
don't mistake a hard bargainer for a good bargainer.

45
00:01:44,520 --> 00:01:48,720
Again, in the model, hard bargainers are, in fact,

46
00:01:48,720 --> 00:01:49,640
not worse.

47
00:01:49,640 --> 00:01:51,200
They're just mediocre.

48
00:01:51,200 --> 00:01:52,560
It's nothing special.

49
00:01:52,560 --> 00:01:55,560
And again, sort of the Donald Trump style

50
00:01:55,560 --> 00:01:58,120
art of the deal, where there's a whole lot of advice saying,

51
00:01:58,120 --> 00:02:00,480
be a hard bargainer.

52
00:02:00,480 --> 00:02:03,520
This is not, in fact, if it were as easy as that,

53
00:02:03,520 --> 00:02:05,000
then everybody would do it.

54
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:06,640
And of course, if it is, and if that's

55
00:02:06,640 --> 00:02:08,760
what everybody were like, then there'd be no deal.

56
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:10,600
So how can that be great?

57
00:02:10,600 --> 00:02:12,560
You might say, well, you want everyone to think

58
00:02:12,560 --> 00:02:13,920
of you as a hard bargainer.

59
00:02:13,920 --> 00:02:17,920
Even that is not clear, as long as people have other options.

60
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:21,160
So if you're the only possible partner in the world,

61
00:02:21,160 --> 00:02:24,640
then you might want the reputation as a hard bargainer.

62
00:02:24,640 --> 00:02:27,040
Then people will just commission it to you without a fight

63
00:02:27,040 --> 00:02:28,520
because they know it's helpless.

64
00:02:28,520 --> 00:02:30,160
But if they can just say, hey, I don't

65
00:02:30,160 --> 00:02:32,800
want to deal with a person that would write a book like this.

66
00:02:32,800 --> 00:02:35,800
Of course, the marketing of the book itself is contradictory.

67
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:38,520
If you really believed in the strategy of art of the deal,

68
00:02:38,520 --> 00:02:40,960
you'd be selling the book for $100,000 a copy.

69
00:02:40,960 --> 00:02:43,160
And just saying, hey, this is what you have to pay for

70
00:02:43,160 --> 00:02:44,840
by great advice.

71
00:02:44,840 --> 00:02:47,280
There's no better advice than this than any other book.

72
00:02:47,280 --> 00:02:48,720
So if you don't want it, too bad.

73
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:50,680
You've got to pay if you want it.

74
00:02:50,680 --> 00:02:54,600
So and yet, the book, of course, is price to sell.

75
00:02:54,600 --> 00:02:55,100
Yes?

76
00:02:55,100 --> 00:02:56,800
You could build a reputation as a hard bargainer

77
00:02:56,800 --> 00:02:58,200
and just give people good deals.

78
00:02:58,200 --> 00:02:59,800
And they'll think that they got one wrong

79
00:02:59,800 --> 00:03:00,600
and they want to make the deal.

80
00:03:00,600 --> 00:03:03,320
There is that possibility as well.

81
00:03:03,320 --> 00:03:05,080
Yet, of course, if they really think

82
00:03:05,080 --> 00:03:06,960
they're not going to get a good deal for you,

83
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:09,040
they won't even want to listen to your sales pitch.

84
00:03:09,040 --> 00:03:11,560
So you don't want it to be too extreme.

85
00:03:14,200 --> 00:03:17,160
So we're not doing any psychology yet.

86
00:03:17,160 --> 00:03:20,280
My personal view is that probably hard bargaining

87
00:03:20,280 --> 00:03:21,760
is overused in general.

88
00:03:21,760 --> 00:03:23,840
And we're not actually at the mixed strategy

89
00:03:23,840 --> 00:03:25,080
in Nash equilibrium.

90
00:03:25,080 --> 00:03:26,880
And I've just seen too many people fighting

91
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:28,120
for no good reason.

92
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:29,480
Where it's like, well, gee, why don't you just

93
00:03:29,480 --> 00:03:30,680
go and cooperate with people?

94
00:03:30,680 --> 00:03:31,880
Wouldn't that be easier?

95
00:03:31,880 --> 00:03:34,360
So I know a lot more cases of people

96
00:03:34,360 --> 00:03:36,920
who have ruined relationships while sticking

97
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:40,440
to some stupid principle no one else ever heard of.

98
00:03:40,440 --> 00:03:43,400
On the theory that other people are trying to exploit them.

99
00:03:43,400 --> 00:03:45,900
And it's like, look, don't they understand this is the rule?

100
00:03:45,900 --> 00:03:47,480
I've never heard of this rule before.

101
00:03:47,480 --> 00:03:50,600
Probably the people you're mad at haven't heard of it either.

102
00:03:50,600 --> 00:03:53,360
So that's just my personal view of things

103
00:03:53,360 --> 00:03:54,560
that I've got.

104
00:03:54,560 --> 00:03:56,840
We're not presenting any evidence here of this.

105
00:03:56,840 --> 00:04:00,960
But in general, part of my general view

106
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:03,160
is that we have a bunch of emotions which perhaps

107
00:04:03,160 --> 00:04:05,200
were functional when we were cavemen,

108
00:04:05,200 --> 00:04:09,360
but are now highly dysfunctional, such as rage.

109
00:04:09,360 --> 00:04:12,200
It's like, look, in general, if you just turn your rage down

110
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:14,600
to one on a scale from 0 to 10, you

111
00:04:14,600 --> 00:04:17,000
would have a more successful life overall.

112
00:04:17,000 --> 00:04:21,080
And yet most people are up at a three or a four.

113
00:04:21,080 --> 00:04:24,280
How often has rage actually accomplished anything for you?

114
00:04:27,160 --> 00:04:28,040
Maybe you can come up.

115
00:04:28,040 --> 00:04:30,560
So no doubt you can come up with some example or other.

116
00:04:30,560 --> 00:04:32,960
But just think about all the times

117
00:04:32,960 --> 00:04:35,000
that your rage has actually caused trouble for you

118
00:04:35,000 --> 00:04:36,160
and achieved nothing.

119
00:04:36,160 --> 00:04:37,600
Or it could be counterproductive.

120
00:04:37,600 --> 00:04:39,000
You've made enemies.

121
00:04:39,000 --> 00:04:42,320
So you lost a deal and you also lost this deal

122
00:04:42,320 --> 00:04:45,880
and you also destroyed the relationship on top of it.

123
00:04:45,880 --> 00:04:48,920
But that's nothing to worry about right now.

124
00:04:48,920 --> 00:04:51,600
Now, this mixed strategy Nash equilibrium is not first best.

125
00:04:51,600 --> 00:04:54,080
First best would be to always be there.

126
00:04:54,080 --> 00:04:55,520
That's first best.

127
00:04:55,520 --> 00:05:00,080
Always be at the outcome that maximizes the sum of payoffs.

128
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:07,000
But the mixed strategy Nash equilibrium

129
00:05:07,000 --> 00:05:09,800
only gives you that outcome 25% of the time.

130
00:05:09,800 --> 00:05:12,200
So it's not terrible.

131
00:05:12,200 --> 00:05:15,280
And as that outcome gets worse and worse,

132
00:05:15,280 --> 00:05:17,760
if we're talking about we both starved to death,

133
00:05:17,760 --> 00:05:19,320
then the probability of that happening

134
00:05:19,320 --> 00:05:21,640
gets smaller and smaller.

135
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:23,600
And remember, since you only get there

136
00:05:23,600 --> 00:05:26,360
if both people have played the hard strategy,

137
00:05:26,360 --> 00:05:27,920
then if that outcome is really bad,

138
00:05:27,920 --> 00:05:31,760
then it's going to be extremely unlikely that would ever occur.

139
00:05:31,760 --> 00:05:33,840
That's balanced by the fact that when it happens,

140
00:05:33,840 --> 00:05:35,280
it's a disaster.

141
00:05:35,280 --> 00:05:38,080
So keep that in mind.

142
00:05:38,080 --> 00:05:41,240
But again, so people like you to think

143
00:05:41,240 --> 00:05:43,240
they're going to play hard.

144
00:05:43,240 --> 00:05:44,840
But again, the problem is precisely

145
00:05:44,840 --> 00:05:46,840
because this perception is value,

146
00:05:46,840 --> 00:05:48,880
it's very common for people to not really

147
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:52,040
be as aggressive in bargaining as they say they'll be.

148
00:05:52,040 --> 00:05:54,880
So I remember a couple of times my wife told someone,

149
00:05:54,880 --> 00:05:58,400
I'll pay that not a penny more.

150
00:05:58,400 --> 00:06:01,880
And then generally, so she says that.

151
00:06:01,880 --> 00:06:04,040
But it's her, maybe you should believe her actually.

152
00:06:04,040 --> 00:06:05,960
But other people don't know that.

153
00:06:05,960 --> 00:06:09,240
And so this is not really all the, so people, oh my god,

154
00:06:09,240 --> 00:06:10,700
she said wouldn't pay a penny more.

155
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:12,720
So well, then let's give her that exact deal.

156
00:06:12,720 --> 00:06:14,280
Or there's no point.

157
00:06:14,280 --> 00:06:18,240
I end up further and further into the discussion.

158
00:06:18,240 --> 00:06:20,360
All right, now what's kind of nice about this model

159
00:06:20,360 --> 00:06:23,120
is that when you do see two people failing to reach a deal

160
00:06:23,120 --> 00:06:25,000
and it's clear that it's mutually beneficial,

161
00:06:25,000 --> 00:06:27,800
it's tempting just to say they're both stupid, which

162
00:06:27,800 --> 00:06:30,960
is a theory we should never dismiss.

163
00:06:30,960 --> 00:06:32,720
You can spend a lot of time in economics,

164
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:35,640
but don't lose the basic juvenile theory

165
00:06:35,640 --> 00:06:37,200
that people that screw up are idiots.

166
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:39,840
That's not always wrong.

167
00:06:39,840 --> 00:06:42,120
But it does open up another possibility,

168
00:06:42,120 --> 00:06:45,080
which is that there was something that was socially

169
00:06:45,080 --> 00:06:47,000
unpleasant that, rare and nevertheless,

170
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:48,500
result from each person just trying

171
00:06:48,500 --> 00:06:51,240
to advance their own interests in a reasonable way.

172
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:55,240
So that is a possibility.

173
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:56,440
So anyway, good enough.

174
00:06:56,440 --> 00:06:57,600
And what is the possibility?

175
00:06:57,600 --> 00:06:58,520
Really just bad luck.

176
00:06:58,520 --> 00:07:00,880
So everyone's randomizing.

177
00:07:00,880 --> 00:07:02,800
And then we happen to get in a situation

178
00:07:02,800 --> 00:07:07,080
where both people randomly chose the outcomes which,

179
00:07:07,080 --> 00:07:11,600
in combination, are bad for both of them.

180
00:07:11,600 --> 00:07:12,840
All right.

181
00:07:12,840 --> 00:07:15,040
OK, questions, questions.

182
00:07:15,040 --> 00:07:16,440
I'm happy.

183
00:07:16,440 --> 00:07:18,920
All right, so remember, our energy

184
00:07:18,920 --> 00:07:22,640
is at a very high level despite all biology,

185
00:07:22,640 --> 00:07:25,080
despite the time of day, despite whatever other work we have

186
00:07:25,080 --> 00:07:27,600
done, because if we are here in this room,

187
00:07:27,600 --> 00:07:29,920
there could only be one possible explanation.

188
00:07:29,920 --> 00:07:31,520
No one studies economics for the money.

189
00:07:31,520 --> 00:07:34,360
They study it for the love of the subject.

190
00:07:34,360 --> 00:07:39,600
And therefore, it follows that we are delighted to be here

191
00:07:39,600 --> 00:07:42,000
and are full of enthusiasm because this is the high point

192
00:07:42,000 --> 00:07:43,840
of our week, correct?

193
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:45,720
Right, OK, good.

194
00:07:45,720 --> 00:07:48,120
All right, now, this bargaining game

195
00:07:48,120 --> 00:07:51,560
is better known as another game, better

196
00:07:51,560 --> 00:07:57,080
known as the chicken game or also named

197
00:07:57,080 --> 00:08:01,200
after two other birds, hock and dub, hock out game.

198
00:08:01,200 --> 00:08:05,040
All right, now, the main change when people represent that game

199
00:08:05,040 --> 00:08:08,800
is they'll usually go and instead of making it zero,

200
00:08:08,800 --> 00:08:10,920
which sounds like the payoffs from just not getting

201
00:08:10,920 --> 00:08:13,800
to a bargain, you'll go and do something like negative 10,

202
00:08:13,800 --> 00:08:18,400
negative 10, showing that when everyone is belligerent

203
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:19,960
or when everyone is uncooperative,

204
00:08:19,960 --> 00:08:22,480
then you get total and you get a disaster.

205
00:08:22,480 --> 00:08:24,600
All right, so that, we also have slight variation.

206
00:08:24,600 --> 00:08:27,040
But this is only in the interpretation.

207
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:30,400
This is not changing anything about the actual game.

208
00:08:30,400 --> 00:08:36,320
All right, so again, but kind of then common to go and relabel.

209
00:08:36,320 --> 00:08:39,840
Instead of Harkins off, we can go and relabel this

210
00:08:39,840 --> 00:08:48,240
as war and peace, war and peace.

211
00:08:48,240 --> 00:08:51,160
And then there's a new interpretation

212
00:08:51,160 --> 00:08:53,800
of the same basic payoff structure, right?

213
00:08:53,800 --> 00:08:56,080
Like, isn't the best world one where all countries are

214
00:08:56,080 --> 00:08:57,440
at peace with each other?

215
00:08:57,440 --> 00:08:58,840
Sounds good, right?

216
00:08:58,840 --> 00:09:00,720
And isn't it a total disaster when countries

217
00:09:00,720 --> 00:09:02,400
go to war with each other?

218
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:03,560
Yes?

219
00:09:03,560 --> 00:09:07,680
So anyone read All Quiet in the Western Front?

220
00:09:07,680 --> 00:09:08,640
Yes.

221
00:09:08,640 --> 00:09:12,160
So now, geez, that's not a sight in high school anymore?

222
00:09:12,160 --> 00:09:13,240
Terrible.

223
00:09:13,240 --> 00:09:14,280
All right, it's one of the few good things

224
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:15,600
to cite in high school.

225
00:09:15,600 --> 00:09:19,400
The graphic descriptions of World War I hospitals, right?

226
00:09:19,400 --> 00:09:22,280
And the list of all the places a man can receive a wound.

227
00:09:22,280 --> 00:09:23,800
All right?

228
00:09:23,800 --> 00:09:25,080
It's almost a quote.

229
00:09:25,080 --> 00:09:27,280
Almost a quote.

230
00:09:27,280 --> 00:09:29,360
Yeah, so terrible.

231
00:09:29,360 --> 00:09:30,000
All right, yes.

232
00:09:30,000 --> 00:09:30,840
So it remains a line.

233
00:09:30,840 --> 00:09:34,600
The hospital alone shows what war is.

234
00:09:34,600 --> 00:09:37,040
Any case, so when there's a war, then there

235
00:09:37,040 --> 00:09:39,160
is total devastation.

236
00:09:39,160 --> 00:09:42,720
But if you are warlike and the other side abuses you,

237
00:09:42,720 --> 00:09:46,440
then the usual story is that they give you great stuff,

238
00:09:46,440 --> 00:09:48,640
and you don't actually have to fight them.

239
00:09:48,640 --> 00:09:53,080
And then they're better off than if they had fought.

240
00:09:53,080 --> 00:09:56,440
Although still, they are unhappy with getting ripped off.

241
00:09:56,440 --> 00:09:58,200
But too bad.

242
00:09:58,200 --> 00:09:59,200
Too bad.

243
00:09:59,200 --> 00:10:00,080
So we have intuition.

244
00:10:00,080 --> 00:10:03,240
So universal peace is usually beneficial,

245
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:06,320
but maybe it's not an equilibrium.

246
00:10:06,320 --> 00:10:07,680
So maybe it's not an equilibrium.

247
00:10:07,680 --> 00:10:10,320
And in fact, as drawn here, it's clearly not, right?

248
00:10:10,320 --> 00:10:14,040
So the equilibrium there are war, peace, and peace, war.

249
00:10:14,040 --> 00:10:16,720
Although that still gives us the optimistic story

250
00:10:16,720 --> 00:10:20,440
that actual full-blown war never happens, right?

251
00:10:20,440 --> 00:10:24,560
Because world war isn't in equilibrium either.

252
00:10:24,560 --> 00:10:27,120
So the classic line in foreign policy is,

253
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:29,320
who will blink first?

254
00:10:29,320 --> 00:10:32,440
Who will blink first?

255
00:10:32,440 --> 00:10:33,520
And blinking is what?

256
00:10:33,520 --> 00:10:35,080
War or peace?

257
00:10:35,080 --> 00:10:35,840
Blinking is peace.

258
00:10:35,840 --> 00:10:37,920
Blinking is like, OK, OK, OK, sorry.

259
00:10:37,920 --> 00:10:39,720
Sorry.

260
00:10:39,720 --> 00:10:41,640
Stop your Berlin airlift.

261
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:42,800
It's my mistake.

262
00:10:42,800 --> 00:10:44,080
What Berlin airlift?

263
00:10:44,080 --> 00:10:44,600
No, no, no.

264
00:10:44,600 --> 00:10:45,720
What are you talking about?

